Not really *many*.  These days a bones file can be downloaded either 
once or twice before it is expired off the server (depending on how many 
bones files exist for that particular version).  Certainly there's more 
chance than on a vanilla setup, I agree.


> Is the information somehow easily retrievably from the bones file?

Not easily, no.  Hearse tries to treat the bones data as a blob and 
doesn't parse it, so I have no code to do such a thing.  This is 'cos 
Hearse has to handle lots of different versions so I've avoided doing 
any parsing work like the plague.

However, even if they were parsed it still won't give you a true picture 
since a good wodge of the 'multiple' bones files would have already been 
downloaded and expired off the server.  Those ones would currently be 
sitting on people's hard drives waiting for a chance to be reuploaded to 
Hearse :)

So whether or not you found a quintuple bones file on Hearse (assuming 
one existed in the first place) would come down to whether it had been 
randomly selected for download recently or not.


> Alexis, would you have the means, time and inclination to check such a 
> thing?

'Fraid not.  That said, if anyone out there feels strongly enough about 
it to write the parsing code, let me know once you've got it working and 
I'll supply you with bones data to run against it.

